# APK Task Author (optional, manual only)

Task author helps a planning agent turn a natural-language request into a reviewable APK task
contract. It is for task authoring, not coding. The primary dogfood target may be Hermes Agent,
but this asset is harness-neutral: any agent that can read repository files and invoke the
repository-local `apkit` CLI through a terminal or shell tool can use it.

## When to use it

- Use only when a human explicitly asks to author, plan, or turn a request into APK task(s), or
  explicitly names `apk-task-author`.
- Never activate automatically from a vague idea, a changed repository, task complexity, or a
  work-loop transition.
- A request to record an idea is not approval to create a task. Return a bounded note or ask
  whether the human wants a concrete task preview.
- It is optional. It adds no CLI command, lifecycle state, worker role, review requirement, or
  completion gate.

## Establish bounded repository truth first

Inspect enough repository truth to avoid inventing or duplicating work. Prefer the repository's
canonical policy, project/scope/architecture docs, task system, current roadmap or milestone,
and the request's likely direct dependencies. Use existing interfaces where useful:

```bash
pnpm exec apkit status --detail
pnpm exec apkit tasks --all
pnpm exec apkit suggest-context "<the human request>"
pnpm exec apkit task deps <relevant-task-id>
pnpm exec apkit context <relevant-task-id> --level 2
```

Read the files selected by those commands; command output is a file-selection aid, not proof of
their contents. Read the task's declared Context files and the canonical docs before deciding
scope. Inspect active tasks first, then archived/completed summaries only when they are relevant
to overlap or historical behavior. Do not scan the whole repository, dump all source files, or
invent a second planning database.

## Detect duplicates and decide the shape

Before drafting, compare the request with active tasks, their allowed paths, goals, acceptance,
dependencies, and likely context. Look for exact duplicates and material overlap, not just title
similarity.

- If an unfinished task already owns the request, report its actual ID and path instead of
  creating a duplicate. Explain the missing distinction if a follow-up task may still be useful.
- If a completed or archived task is the relevant history, do not rewrite or reopen it. A defect
  in completed work becomes a fresh corrective task with explicit regression scope.
- If the request has one coherent observable outcome, draft one task.
- If it spans unrelated outcomes, ownership boundaries, or independently verifiable vertical
  slices, do not hide that breadth in a horizontal catch-all. Propose `apk-task-split` as the
  next planning operation or present a bounded multi-task preview with explicit dependency edges.
- Treat ID-based dependencies as graph facts. Distinguish an archived/done prerequisite from an
  active, blocked, or missing dependency and surface any unresolved graph risk.

## Ask only material clarification questions

Repository truth owns implementation details. Ask the human one question at a time only when a
material choice cannot be resolved from the repository, such as product outcome, ownership,
security boundary, destructive/external effect, compatibility promise, or an irreversible scope
choice. Before each question state:

1. what repository evidence already establishes;
2. what material ambiguity remains; and
3. how the answer changes the proposed task or dependency graph.

Do not ask the human to choose filenames, libraries, test structure, or coding tactics that the
future implementation agent can decide inside the approved contract. Do not convert silence,
elapsed time, a model preference, or a vague "sounds good" into approval.

## Build one concrete preview before any write

Present a complete preview in canonical task vocabulary. For each proposed task include, as
applicable:

- title, type/template, mode, lane, risk, and whether it is parallelizable;
- one outcome-focused goal and a narrow scope;
- exact context files, allowed files, and forbidden files;
- dependency IDs or clearly labeled symbolic edges for a multi-task preview;
- ordered implementation steps and observable acceptance criteria;
- structured verification checks with requiredness, environment, and profile;
- correctness assumptions, invariants, required evidence, review questions, and counterexample
  searches when they materially protect the outcome;
- documentation updates and notes, including explicit unknowns or bounded best-effort limits.

Check that allowed and forbidden paths do not overlap, the verification is runnable or honestly
marked manual/live, the task is small enough to review, and the contract does not authorize the
planning agent to implement anything. State the proposed files and graph effects plainly.

## Explicit approval is required before task creation

Ask for explicit approval of the concrete preview, including its task count, paths, dependencies,
acceptance, verification, and documentation effects. No task file may be written before that
approval. A human approving discussion in general, invoking this skill, or answering an unrelated
question is not approval of a concrete preview.

After approval, create only the accepted task contract(s) through canonical APK facilities:

```bash
pnpm exec apkit task create --title "..." --scope "..." --allowed "..." \
  --mode <mode> --lane <lane> --risk <risk> --context "..." \
  --forbidden "..." --depends "..." --verification-json '<structured checks>'
```

Use the actual supported `apkit task create` flags for the complete preview. Never allocate an ID
yourself, hand-write `.tasks/<id>-<slug>.md`, or rewrite an existing task to make the graph fit.
For a large request, hand the approved planning target to `apk-task-split` instead of creating a
giant contract. Report every actual ID and path returned by canonical creation.

## Validate and stop at planning

Immediately after creation, validate the actual result:

```bash
pnpm exec apkit lint --json
pnpm exec apkit task deps <new-task-id>
```

Inspect the created contract and report any remaining graph, path, or verification blockers. If
validation fails, correct only the newly created unfinished contract through canonical task
facilities; do not silently broaden scope or touch completed history. If no approval was given,
create nothing.

Stop after reporting the validated task ID/path and blockers. Do not claim or start the task, issue
an `apk work` package, implement product/docs changes, run implementation verification, fabricate
evidence, request review, pass a gate, or mark the task done. The normal APK work loop owns those
later actions.

## Relation to other optional skills

- `apk-project-grill` explores project, milestone, or subsystem direction before task planning.
- `apk-task-author` turns an approved human request into a concrete task-contract preview and
  canonical creation.
- `apk-task-split` decomposes a genuinely large target into proposed vertical-slice contracts.
- `apk-task-grill` clarifies one existing task after creation and before implementation.
- `apk-prototype` handles one bounded throwaway feasibility experiment, not durable task writing.

These assets are separate and manually invoked. One does not auto-activate or become required
because another was used.

## Hard boundaries

- No product/runtime code, migrations, dependency changes, provider SDKs, Hermes runtime APIs,
  model routing, daemon, remote service, or user-global skill installation.
- No writes before explicit approval of the concrete preview.
- No direct task-file writes, duplicate task database, automatic backlog scan, or invented state.
- No implementation, claim, worker handoff, verification evidence, review, gate, or done action.
- Completed task history and evidence are immutable; create a fresh corrective task when needed.

## Invocation note for harnesses

This asset is plain Markdown shipped inside the installed package under
`dist/core/templates/skills/apk-task-author/SKILL.md.hbs`. A harness with native skill discovery
can reference the installed asset or a user-installed copy. A harness without native discovery
must read the installed asset explicitly when a human invokes task author. APK adds no installer,
model routing, provider integration, or user-global skill directory write.
